\hypertarget{4773c27b}{}
\hypertarget{part-1-the-beta-binomial-model}{%
\section{Part 1) The beta-binomial
model}\label{part-1-the-beta-binomial-model}}

Your friend has set up a website for her new business. So far
\(N = 115\) potential customers has visited her site, but only \(y = 4\)
customers have completed a purchase. To plan her future investments, she
asks you for help to compute the probability that at least one of the
next \(N^∗ = 20\) customers will make a purchase. You decide to model
the problem using the beta-binomial model with a uniform prior
distribution on the probability of making a purchase
\(\theta \in [0, 1]\):

\[\begin{align}
\theta &∼ \text{Beta}(a_0, b_0) \\
y|\theta &∼ \text{Binomial}(N, \theta) \\
\end{align}\]

where \(a_0 = b_0 = 1\)

\hypertarget{6bf74f7d}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\NormalTok{a0 }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ b0 }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{1}
\NormalTok{y }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{4}
\NormalTok{N }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{115}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\hypertarget{48c1e8aa}{}
\hypertarget{task-11-compute-the-prior-mean-of-theta-and-provide-a-95-interval-for-the-prior}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 1.1) Compute the prior mean of
\(\theta\) and provide a 95\% interval for the
prior}{Task 1.1) Compute the prior mean of \textbackslash theta and provide a 95\% interval for the prior}}\label{task-11-compute-the-prior-mean-of-theta-and-provide-a-95-interval-for-the-prior}}

As the Beta distribution is a distribution in the interval \([0,1]\),
and is symmetric and equiprobable over it when \(a=b=1\), it is easy to
see that the prior mean is 0.5. Formally, the mean of the beta
distribution is \(\frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta}\), and we can calculate
it easily as:
\[ \mathbb{E}[\theta] = \frac{\alpha}{\alpha + \beta} = 0.5 \]

The same reasoning can be done for the 95\% credibility interval. In
this case, as it is symmetric, it corresponds to
\(\theta\in[0.025, 0.975]\)

\hypertarget{b6cd277a}{}
\hypertarget{task-12-compute-the-posterior-mean-of-theta-and-provide-a-95-credibility-interval-for-the-posterior}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 1.2) Compute the posterior mean of
\(\theta\) and provide a 95\% credibility interval for the
posterior}{Task 1.2) Compute the posterior mean of \textbackslash theta and provide a 95\% credibility interval for the posterior}}\label{task-12-compute-the-posterior-mean-of-theta-and-provide-a-95-credibility-interval-for-the-posterior}}

For this, we must compute the posterior
\(p(\theta|y)\propto p(y|\theta)p(\theta)\). As the \(\beta\)
distribution is a conjugate distribution to a binomial distribution, the
posterior is likewise a Beta distribution. In the exercises and in
class, it was shown that the resulting posterior distribution will
follow a beta distribution as follows: \[ \begin{align}
p(\theta|y) &= \text{Beta}(\theta | a_0 + y, b_0 + N - y) \\
&= \text{Beta}(\theta | 1 + 4, 1 + 115 - 4) 
&= \text{Beta}(\theta | 5, 112) \end{align}
\]

\hypertarget{2f3999b2}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\NormalTok{a\_post }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ a0 }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ y}
\NormalTok{b\_post }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ b0 }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ N }\OperatorTok{{-}}\NormalTok{ y}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\leavevmode\vadjust pre{\hypertarget{34a86111}{}}%
Let \(y^*\) denote the number of purchases during the next \(N^*=20\)
visits.

\hypertarget{task-13-compute-and-plot-the-posterior-predictive-distribution-for-y-given-y--4}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 1.3) Compute and plot the posterior
predictive distribution for \(y^∗\) given
\(y = 4\).}{Task 1.3) Compute and plot the posterior predictive distribution for y\^{}∗ given y = 4.}}\label{task-13-compute-and-plot-the-posterior-predictive-distribution-for-y-given-y--4}}

For this task, we must compute
\(p(y^*|y,\theta,N^*) = \int p(y^*|\theta)p(\theta|y) \partial\theta\).
When inserting the PMF for the binomial and beta distributions, it
simplifies a such that the integral only has the functional form of the
beta distribution left, and so the above integral will evalute to:

\[p(y^*|y,N^*) = \int p(y^*|\theta)p(\theta|y) d\theta = \binom{N^*}{k}\frac{B(\alpha+k,\beta+N^*-k)}{B(\alpha,\beta)},\text{ where } B(a, b) = \frac{\Gamma(a)\Gamma(b)}{\Gamma(a+b)} \]

In this case, we have that \(N^* = 20\), and so \(k\in[0,20]\).

In the plot, we see that the majority of the probability mass is
centered around 0 and 1. We expect this, as only originally 4 out of the
115 customers in the original data would make a purcahse.

\hypertarget{c5724e8f}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\KeywordTok{def}\NormalTok{ beta\_norm(a, b):}
    \ControlFlowTok{return}\NormalTok{ gamma(a) }\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ gamma(b) }\OperatorTok{/}\NormalTok{ gamma(a }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ b)}

\NormalTok{N\_star }\OperatorTok{=} \DecValTok{20}
\NormalTok{k }\OperatorTok{=} \BuiltInTok{list}\NormalTok{(}\BuiltInTok{range}\NormalTok{(N\_star }\OperatorTok{+} \DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{))}
\NormalTok{post\_pred\_probs }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ np.array([comb(N\_star, k\_) }\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ (beta\_norm(k\_ }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ a\_post, b\_post }\OperatorTok{+}\NormalTok{ N\_star }\OperatorTok{{-}}\NormalTok{ k\_)}\OperatorTok{/}\NormalTok{beta\_norm(a\_post, b\_post)) }\ControlFlowTok{for}\NormalTok{ k\_ }\KeywordTok{in}\NormalTok{ k])}

\BuiltInTok{print}\NormalTok{(}\StringTok{"Sum of predictive probs:"}\NormalTok{, post\_pred\_probs.}\BuiltInTok{sum}\NormalTok{())}

\NormalTok{plt.figure(figsize}\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{(}\DecValTok{10}\NormalTok{, }\DecValTok{6}\NormalTok{))}
\NormalTok{plt.bar(k, post\_pred\_probs, color}\OperatorTok{=}\StringTok{"blue"}\NormalTok{, alpha}\OperatorTok{=}\FloatTok{0.7}\NormalTok{)}
\NormalTok{plt.xlabel(}\StringTok{"Number of successes (k)"}\NormalTok{)}
\NormalTok{plt.ylabel(}\StringTok{"Posterior predictive probability"}\NormalTok{)}
\NormalTok{plt.title(}\StringTok{"Posterior Predictive Distribution (Beta{-}Binomial)"}\NormalTok{)}
\NormalTok{plt.xticks(k)}
\NormalTok{plt.grid(axis}\OperatorTok{=}\StringTok{"y"}\NormalTok{)}
\NormalTok{plt.show()}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\begin{verbatim}
Sum of predictive probs: 0.9999999999999996
\end{verbatim}

\includegraphics{vertopal_969b52de0f6b497b89d50245216b8b60/3e4227a5219ed7d7baca9e4bc0508aa1c97ec35c.png}

\hypertarget{de543b12}{}
\hypertarget{task-14-what-is-the-posterior-predictive-probability-that-at-least-one-of-the-next-n-20-customers-will-make-a-purchase}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 1.4) What is the posterior predictive
probability that at least one of the next \(N^∗= 20\) customers will
make a
purchase?}{Task 1.4) What is the posterior predictive probability that at least one of the next N\^{}∗= 20 customers will make a purchase?}}\label{task-14-what-is-the-posterior-predictive-probability-that-at-least-one-of-the-next-n-20-customers-will-make-a-purchase}}

We the predictive posterior distribution, this is easy as must simply
sum up for \(y = k\), for \(k\geq 1\):
\[ p(y^* >= 1|y, N^*) = \sum_{k=1}^{N^*} p(y^* = k|y, N^*) = \sum_{k=1}^{N^*} \binom{N^*}{k}\frac{B(\alpha+k,\beta+N^*-k)}{B(\alpha,\beta)} \approx 55.44\%\]

\hypertarget{b109fcef}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\NormalTok{np.}\BuiltInTok{sum}\NormalTok{(post\_pred\_probs[}\DecValTok{1}\NormalTok{:]).item()}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\begin{verbatim}
0.5543908502644233
\end{verbatim}

\hypertarget{d54cf84c}{}
\hypertarget{task-15-compute-mean-and-variance-of-the-posterior-predictive-distribution-for-y}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 1.5) Compute mean and variance of the
posterior predictive distribution for
\(y^∗\).}{Task 1.5) Compute mean and variance of the posterior predictive distribution for y\^{}∗.}}\label{task-15-compute-mean-and-variance-of-the-posterior-predictive-distribution-for-y}}

The mean of the binomial distribution is simply
\(\mathbb{E}[y^*|y,N^*] = np\). Here, however, \(\theta\) is uncertain
and integrated out, so \(y^*|y,N^*\) follows a posterior predictive
(beta-binomial) distribution. So we must do it the old fashioned way,
where the mean of a stochastic discrete random variable is computed as
\[
\mathbb{E}[y^*|y,N^*] = \sum_{k=0}^{N^*} k\,p(y^*=k|y,N^*) \approx 0.855.
\]

Likewise, the variance is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{V}[y^*|y,N^*]
&= \mathbb{E}\!\left[(y^*-\mu)^2 \mid y,N^*\right] \\
&= \mathbb{E}\!\left[(y^*)^2 \mid y,N^*\right] - \mu^2,\quad
\mu=\mathbb{E}[y^*|y,N^*] \\
&\approx 0.95.
\end{aligned}
\]

\hypertarget{dacdba38}{}
\begin{Shaded}
\begin{Highlighting}[]
\NormalTok{mu }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ np.}\BuiltInTok{sum}\NormalTok{(np.arange(}\BuiltInTok{len}\NormalTok{(post\_pred\_probs)) }\OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ post\_pred\_probs).item()}
\NormalTok{var }\OperatorTok{=}\NormalTok{ np.}\BuiltInTok{sum}\NormalTok{((np.arange(}\BuiltInTok{len}\NormalTok{(post\_pred\_probs)) }\OperatorTok{{-}}\NormalTok{ mu)}\OperatorTok{**}\DecValTok{2} \OperatorTok{*}\NormalTok{ post\_pred\_probs).item()}
\NormalTok{mu, var}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

\begin{verbatim}
(0.8547008547008542, 0.9499152480464949)
\end{verbatim}

\hypertarget{eaf32382}{}
\hypertarget{part-2-linear-gaussian-systems}{%
\section{Part 2) Linear Gaussian
systems}\label{part-2-linear-gaussian-systems}}

Let \(z_1,z_2\in\mathbb{R}^2\) and \(y\in\mathbb{R}\) be random
variables and consider the following linear Gaussian system:
\[ \begin{align}
z_1 &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, v\mathbf{I}) \\
z_2|z_1 &\sim \mathcal{N}(z_1, v\mathbf{I}) \\
y|z_2 &\sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{a}^\top z_2, \sigma^2),
\end{align}\]

where \(\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{R}^2\) is a constant. The joint
distribution of \((y,z_2, z_1)\) is given by
\[ p(y,z_2,z_1) = p(y|z_2)p(z_2|z_1)p(z_1) \]

\hypertarget{7f1294bf}{}
\hypertarget{task-21-determine-the-distribution-py}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 2.1) Determine the distribution
\(p(y)\)}{Task 2.1) Determine the distribution p(y)}}\label{task-21-determine-the-distribution-py}}

To determine this, we must use probability rules to determine the
expression for \(p(y)\). If we start with equation (1) to (3), it would
be beneficial to compute an expression for \(p(z_2)\) first.

We see that we can write \(z_2 = z_1 + e_2\) where
\(e_2\sim\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, v\mathbf{I})\). When we do an affine
transformation of multivariate Gaussian distributions, we get yet
another Guassian distribution

\[ \begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[z_1 + e_2] &= \mathbb{E}[z_1] + \mathbb{E}[e_2] = \mathbf{0} + \mathbf{0} = \mathbf{0} \\
\mathbb{V}[z_1 + e_2] &= \mathbb{V}[z_1] + \mathbb{V}[e_2] = v\mathbf{I} + v\mathbf{I} = 2v\mathbf{I}
\end{align*} \]

Note, in the above the covariance from the variance expression is left
out as it zeros out. From above we now see that
\(z_2 \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},2v\mathbf{I})\).

Now we can similar do \(y = \mathbf{a}^\top z_2 + e_y\) where
\(e_y = \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)\).

\[ \begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[\mathbf{a}^\top z_2 + e_y] &= \mathbf{a}^\top\mathbb{E}[z_2] + \mathbb{E}[e_y] = \mathbf{a}^\top\mathbf{0} + 0 = 0 \\
\mathbb{V}[\mathbf{a}^\top z_2 + e_y] &= \mathbb{V}[\mathbf{a}^\top z_2] + \mathbb{V}[e_y] = \mathbf{a}^\top 2v\mathbf{I}\mathbf{a} + \sigma^2 = 2v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2 + \sigma^2,
\end{align*} \]

And so we arrive with
\(p(y) = \mathcal{N}(y|0, 2v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2 + \sigma^2)\)

\hypertarget{e10049e0}{}
\hypertarget{task-22-determine-the-distribution-pyz_2z_1}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 2.2) Determine the distribution
\(p(y,z_2|z_1)\)}{Task 2.2) Determine the distribution p(y,z\_2\textbar z\_1)}}\label{task-22-determine-the-distribution-pyz_2z_1}}

We can write out this joint distribution as
\(p(y,z_2|z_1) = p(z_2|z_1)p(y|z_2)\). This looks like the linear system
described in the book by Murphy, where the joint distribution would have
mean and covariance matrix described as

\[ \begin{align}
\mathbf{\mu} &= \begin{bmatrix} \mu_{z_2|z_1} \\ \mathbf{a}^\top \mu_{z_2|z_1} \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} z_1 \\ \mathbf{a}^\top z_1 \end{bmatrix} \\
\mathbf{\Sigma} &= \begin{bmatrix} v\mathbf{I} & v\mathbf{I}\mathbf{a} \\ v\mathbf{I}\mathbf{a}^\top& \sigma^2 + \mathbf{a}(v\mathbf{I})\mathbf{a}^\top \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} v\mathbf{I} & v\mathbf{a} \\ v\mathbf{a}^\top& \sigma^2 + v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2 \end{bmatrix} \\
\end{align} \]

and so the distribution of \$p(y,z\_2\textbar z\_1) =
\textbackslash mathcal\{N\}\textbackslash left(
\textbackslash begin\{bmatrix\} z\_2 \textbackslash{} y
\textbackslash end\{bmatrix\} \textbackslash middle\textbar{}
\textbackslash begin\{bmatrix\} z\_1 \textbackslash{}
\textbackslash mathbf\{a\}\^{}\textbackslash top z\_1
\textbackslash end\{bmatrix\}, \textbackslash begin\{bmatrix\}
v\textbackslash mathbf\{I\} \& v\textbackslash mathbf\{a\}
\textbackslash{} v\textbackslash mathbf\{a\}\^{}\textbackslash top\&
\textbackslash sigma\^{}2 +
v\textbar\textbar\textbackslash mathbf\{a\}\textbar\textbar\_2\^{}2
\textbackslash end\{bmatrix\} \textbackslash right) \$

\hypertarget{71218fc1}{}
\hypertarget{task-23-determine-the-distribution-pyz_1}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 2.3) Determine the distribution
\(p(y|z_1)\)}{Task 2.3) Determine the distribution p(y\textbar z\_1)}}\label{task-23-determine-the-distribution-pyz_1}}

We were smart in task 2.2 to write out the linear Gaussian system in
such a way, that we can easily inspect it to determine the distribution,
which is the lower element of the \(\mu\) matrix, and the bottom right
of the covariance matrix \(\mathbf{\mathbf{\Sigma}}\):

\[ p(y|z_1) = \mathcal{N}\left(y|\mathbf{a}^\top z_1, \sigma^2 + v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2\right) \]

\hypertarget{79b4fa37}{}
\hypertarget{task-24-determine-the-distribution-pz_1y}{%
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{Task 2.4) Determine the distribution
\(p(z_1|y)\)}{Task 2.4) Determine the distribution p(z\_1\textbar y)}}\label{task-24-determine-the-distribution-pz_1y}}

Writing this out with \emph{Bayes\textquotesingle{} Theorem}, we get
\(p(z_1|y) = \frac{p(y|z_1)p(z_1)}{p(y)}\), where the \emph{marginal
likelihood} \(p(y)\) is simply normalizing it to 1. In the book Murphy,
we also get the closed form solution on the form below, where I have
appropriately substitued the terms which may be relevant from task 2.3
(the variance for example \(\Sigma_y\) which is the varince from task
2.3, and \emph{not} just the variance of \(y\) ).

\[ \begin{align}
p(\mathbf{z_1}|\mathbf{y}) &= \mathcal{N}(z_1|\mathbf{\mu}_{z_1|y}, \mathbf{\Sigma}_{z_1|y}) \\
\mathbf{\Sigma}^{-1}_{z_1|y} &= (v\mathbf{I})^{-1} + \mathbf{a}(\sigma^2 + v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2)^{-1}\mathbf{a} ^\top \\
\mathbf{\mu}_{z_1|y} &= \mathbf{\Sigma}_{z_1|y}\left[ \mathbf{a}(\sigma^2 + v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2)^{-1}(y) + v\mathbf{I}\mathbf{0} \right] = \mathbf{\Sigma}_{z_1|y}\left[ \frac{y\mathbf{a}}{\sigma^2 + v||\mathbf{a}||_2^2} \right],
\end{align} \]

which is the final posterior distribution.

\hypertarget{0358e9b0}{}
\hypertarget{part-3-poisson-gamma}{%
\section{Part 3) Poisson-Gamma}\label{part-3-poisson-gamma}}

\end{document}
